\section{Imports}

The export needs CSV writing, temporary directories for code state
extraction, and zip file creation.
We import [[get_commits]], [[filter_internal_runs]],
[[BLOCK_SEP]], and [[get_tag_map]] from the commits module
rather than reimplementing the commit-walking logic.
The tag map lets us populate the [[X-Tag]] column in the
event table.
Reassembling a run from its two commits is likewise the commits
module's job (\cref{sec:run-join}): [[get_run_trailers]] and
[[merge_run_trailers]] hand us the same complete run block that a
single-commit log would have contained, so everything below parses
one format.
Deciding what a run \emph{is} comes from there too: [[is_internal_run]]
names the plumbing this export drops, and [[is_review_run]] the
reflection it exports under an event type of its own
(\cref{sec:review-events}).
Importing both rather than testing the command string here is what
keeps the dataset agreeing with what [[list]] and [[play]] show---the
three views classify a run by asking the same function
(\cref{sec:filter-learnlog}).
Every Git invocation goes through [[LearnlogRepo.git]]
(\cref{sec:git-bytes}), so nothing here imports [[subprocess]];
[[sys]] is imported for the single message the restorer writes to
standard error (\cref{sec:reconstruct}).

