\section{Code state identification}

ProgSnap2 requires a snapshot of the source files at each event.
Rather than duplicating identical file trees for every run within
a commit, we use the Git tree hash to identify unique states:
commits with the same tree hash share the same code state.

We assign incrementing integer IDs to each unique tree hash,
starting from~0.
The caller passes a mutable [[seen_trees]] dict that maps tree
hashes to IDs across all commits.
We return both the ID and the tree hash so that callers can
reuse it without a second [[git rev-parse]] call.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def get_code_state_id(repo, commit_hash, seen_trees):
    """Get or assign a CodeStateID for a commit.

    Uses the Git tree hash to deduplicate: commits with
    identical file trees share the same ID.  ``seen_trees``
    is a mutable dict mapping tree hashes to integer IDs.
    Returns ``(state_id, tree_hash)``.
    """
    result = repo.git(
        "rev-parse", f"{commit_hash}^{{tree}}"
    )
    tree_hash = result.stdout.strip()

    if tree_hash not in seen_trees:
        seen_trees[tree_hash] = len(seen_trees)

    return seen_trees[tree_hash], tree_hash
@


\section{Extracting code state files}
\label{sec:extract-code-state}

To populate [[CodeStates/]], we extract the file tree for each
unique code state.
[[git archive]] hands us the whole tree as a single tar stream, which
we unpack into the target directory---one command per state, rather
than a checkout per state that would disturb the work tree we are
reading.

A tar stream is bytes, and that used to be the reason this function
bypassed [[repo.git()]] and called [[subprocess.run]] itself.
The bypass cost more than the decoding it avoided.
Without the wrapper's failure handling, a [[git archive]] that failed
returned quietly, and the caller wrote an \emph{empty} directory into
[[CodeStates/]].
Nothing downstream can distinguish that from a genuinely empty working
tree, so the export looked successful while the dataset claimed the
student had deleted every file---and reading such a dataset back does
exactly what it says: [[restore_code_state]]
(\cref{sec:reconstruct}) treats a code state as the whole tree, so
playback deletes the reconstructed work tree file by file.

Now that the wrapper can return bytes (\cref{sec:git-bytes}),
extraction is an ordinary [[repo.git()]] call and a failure raises
[[GitError]] out of the export.
Stopping is the right answer here even though a missing code state
would only spoil part of the dataset: a dataset that lies about what
the student wrote is worse than one that was never written, because
only the one that was never written gets noticed.
The timeout is the tree-sized one for the same reason [[git add .]]
uses it---the command reads every file in the tree.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def extract_code_state(repo, commit_hash, state_dir):
    """Extract the file tree of a commit into a directory.

    Uses ``git archive`` to produce a tar stream and unpacks
    it into ``state_dir``.  Raises ``GitError`` if Git cannot
    produce the archive, rather than leaving an empty state
    behind.
    """
    state_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    result = repo.git(
        "archive", "--format=tar", commit_hash,
        text=False, timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
    )
    with tarfile.open(
        fileobj=io.BytesIO(result.stdout)
    ) as tar:
        tar.extractall(
            path=str(state_dir),
            filter="data",
        )
@

Unpacking needs no path check of its own.
[[filter="data"]] is tarfile's strictest extraction policy: it refuses
members whose names are absolute, escape the destination, or link
outside it, which is precisely the guarantee the read-back path has to
establish by hand (\cref{sec:reconstruct}) because there the names
arrive from a zip index that no filter has vetted.

<<progsnap2 test functions>>=
def test_get_code_state_id_deduplicates(workdir):
    from learnlog.progsnap2 import get_code_state_id
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    seen = {}
    # The initial commit and a run without file changes
    # should share the same tree hash.
    t1 = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0)
    repo.begin_run("hello.py", [], t1)
    commits_result = repo.git(
        "log", "--format=%H", "--reverse"
    )
    hashes = commits_result.stdout.strip().split("\n")
    hashes = [h for h in hashes if h]
    # All commits without file changes share the same tree
    ids = [
        get_code_state_id(repo, h, seen)[0]
        for h in hashes
    ]
    assert len(set(ids)) == 1

def test_get_code_state_id_new_for_changes(workdir):
    from learnlog.progsnap2 import get_code_state_id
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    seen = {}
    t1 = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0)
    repo.begin_run("hello.py", [], t1)
    id1 = get_code_state_id(
        repo, repo.git(
            "log", "-1", "--format=%H"
        ).stdout.strip(), seen
    )[0]
    (workdir / "hello.py").write_text('print("v2")\n')
    t2 = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 5)
    repo.begin_run("hello.py", [], t2)
    id2 = get_code_state_id(
        repo, repo.git(
            "log", "-1", "--format=%H"
        ).stdout.strip(), seen
    )[0]
    assert id1 != id2

def test_extract_code_state(workdir):
    from learnlog.progsnap2 import extract_code_state
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    commit = repo.git(
        "log", "-1", "--format=%H"
    ).stdout.strip()
    out = workdir / "state_out"
    extract_code_state(repo, commit, out)
    assert out.is_dir()
    # Should contain hello.py from the workdir fixture
    files = list(out.iterdir())
    assert len(files) >= 1
@

A commit Git cannot read must not pass for a commit with no files.
The empty directory is the dangerous outcome, so the test asserts both
that the failure is raised and that no state was left behind for a
caller to mistake for a snapshot:

<<progsnap2 test functions>>=
def test_extract_code_state_raises_on_git_failure(workdir):
    from learnlog.progsnap2 import extract_code_state
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    out = workdir / "state_out"
    with pytest.raises(GitError):
        extract_code_state(repo, "0" * 40, out)
    assert list(out.iterdir()) == []
@


\section{Writing the ProgSnap2 dataset}

The main export function walks the commit history, builds the
CSV tables, extracts code states, and packages everything into
a zip file.

The algorithm has three phases:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Walk commits to build the event rows, collect
  unique code state tree hashes, and accumulate I/O
  resource files.
\item Extract each unique code state into a temporary directory.
\item Write the zip file with [[DatasetMetadata.csv]],
  [[MainTable.csv]], [[CodeStates/]], and [[Resources/]]
  entries.
\end{enumerate}

We separate these phases because code state extraction requires
I/O to a temporary directory, and we want to minimise the
lifetime of that temporary data.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def do_export_progsnap2(repo, output_path,
                        filter_internal_flag=True):
    """Export the learnlog repository as a ProgSnap2 zip.

    Creates a zip file at ``output_path`` containing
    ``DatasetMetadata.csv``, ``MainTable.csv``, a
    ``CodeStates/`` directory with source file snapshots,
    and a ``Resources/`` directory with I/O text files.
    """
    <<collect events and code states>>
    <<write progsnap2 zip file>>
@

\subsection{Collecting events}

We walk commits in chronological order, parsing each commit
message into run blocks.
For each commit we determine the code state and generate events:
a [[File.Edit]] event when the tree hash changes from the
previous commit, and one event per run block---[[Run.Program]] for
the student's own programs, [[X-Review]] for the [[play]] and
[[list]] invocations with which they looked back at them.

The [[Order]] column provides a global sequence number across
all events, scoped per subject (the [[EventOrderScope]] metadata
field).
Since learnlog records one student at a time, a single counter
suffices.

<<collect events and code states>>=
commits = get_commits(repo)
tag_map = get_tag_map(repo)
trailers = get_run_trailers(repo)
seen_trees = {}
events = []
resource_files = []
order = 0
prev_tree = None

for commit in commits:
    commit_hash = commit["hash"]
    commit_tags = ";".join(
        tag_map.get(commit_hash, [])
    )

    <<get subject and code state for commit>>
    <<optionally emit File.Edit event>>
    <<emit run events>>
@

We extract the author name as [[SubjectID]], since each learnlog
repository belongs to one student.
For timestamps, we use both [[ServerTimestamp]] and
[[ClientTimestamp]] set to the same value because learnlog
timestamps come from the local clock.
The run's finish time is a different kind of fact: it is not an
alternative view of the event timestamp, but the other endpoint of the
execution interval.
We therefore preserve it in a custom [[X-EndTime]] column instead of
overloading ProgSnap2's standard timestamp fields.

<<get subject and code state for commit>>=
author_result = repo.git(
    "log", "-1", "--format=%an", commit_hash,
)
subject_id = author_result.stdout.strip()

code_state_id, current_tree = get_code_state_id(
    repo, commit_hash, seen_trees,
)
@

A [[File.Edit]] event signals that the student changed their
source code.
We emit one whenever the current tree hash differs from the
previous commit's tree hash.
The initial commit also gets a [[File.Edit]] because writing
code for the first time is an edit from nothing---and
[[prev_tree]] starts as [[None]], which never equals a real
tree hash.

<<optionally emit File.Edit event>>=
if current_tree != prev_tree:
    order += 1
    events.append({
        "EventType": "File.Edit",
        "EventID": order,
        "Order": order,
        "SubjectID": subject_id,
        "CodeStateID": code_state_id,
        "ParentEventID": "",
        "ServerTimestamp": commit["date"],
        "ClientTimestamp": commit["date"],
        "ProgramResult": "",
        "X-ExitCode": "",
        "X-Script": "",
        "X-Arguments": "",
        "X-ExceptionType": "",
        "X-ExceptionMessage": "",
        "ProgramOutput": "",
        "ProgramInput": "",
        "X-Tag": commit_tags,
        "X-CommitHash": commit_hash,
    })
@

For each run block we emit a [[Run.Program]] event with the
parsed metadata.
The message we parse is the commit's own message with the run's
trailer merged back in, so a run that finished contributes its exit
code, timing, and I/O to the same event as its header---the
[[Run.Program]] event stays anchored to the commit that holds the
code state the run executed.
When [[filter_internal_flag]] is set, we skip blocks that record
learnlog's internal activity (the same test as [[filter_internal_runs]],
but at the parsed-block level rather than raw text).

After emitting the run event, we generate its child events---the
chunked I/O of a program run, or the view trace of a review run.
The spec recommends [[ParentEventID]] to link child events
to their parent (v8, \S4.3.6, lines~902--912), so each child
event references its parent run's [[EventID]].
We save that ID in [[run_event_id]] before the order counter
advances past the children.

A review run is a run in every respect this table can see: it has a
script, arguments, a start and an end, and it either finished or it
did not.
So it fills the same columns, and only the [[EventType]] differs.
That difference is the point.
The analysis in \cref{sec:group-pairs} reads the stream as an
alternation of [[File.Edit]] and [[Run.Program]], and a student who
edits, pages back through yesterday's failures, and then runs would---
were the review a [[Run.Program]]---have the review credited with the
edit and the real run recorded as a re-run of unchanged code.
Marking it [[X-Review]] is the whole fix, because the pairing loop
dispatches on [[EventType]] and has no branch for anything else: an
event type it does not know passes through it untouched.
An analysis tool that has never heard of [[X-Review]] behaves the same
way, which is the property the [[X-]] prefix exists to promise.

<<emit run events>>=
body = repo.git(
    "log", "-1", "--format=%B", commit_hash,
).stdout.rstrip("\n")
body = merge_run_trailers(body, trailers)

runs = parse_commit_runs(body)
for run in runs:
    if filter_internal_flag and is_internal_run(run):
        continue

    order += 1
    run_event_id = order
    review = is_review_run(run)
    program_result = classify_run_result(run)
    exc_type, exc_msg = parse_exception_line(
        run["exception_info"]
    )

    timestamp = run["start_time"] or commit["date"]

    events.append({
        "EventType": "X-Review" if review else "Run.Program",
        "EventID": order,
        "Order": order,
        "SubjectID": subject_id,
        "CodeStateID": code_state_id,
        "ParentEventID": "",
        "ServerTimestamp": timestamp,
        "ClientTimestamp": timestamp,
        "ProgramResult": program_result,
        "X-EndTime": run["end_time"] or "",
        "X-ExitCode": run["exit_code"] or "",
        "X-Script": run["script"] or "",
        "X-Arguments": run["arguments"] or "",
        "X-ExceptionType": exc_type or "",
        "X-ExceptionMessage": exc_msg or "",
        "ProgramOutput": "",
        "ProgramInput": "",
        "X-Tag": commit_tags,
        "X-CommitHash": commit_hash,
    })

    if review:
        <<emit X-Review.View events>>
    else:
        <<emit I/O events for run>>

prev_tree = current_tree
@

The ProgSnap2 spec defines [[ProgramInput]] and
[[ProgramOutput]] as URL-typed columns that reference files
in the [[Resources/]] directory (v8, \S4.4.2,
lines~1224--1261).
Each I/O chunk is written to a separate file named
[[io_{EventID}.txt]] under [[Resources/]], and the CSV
cell contains the relative path to that file.
Using the standard columns rather than custom inline
ones ensures that generic ProgSnap2 analysis tools can
read the I/O data without special handling---custom
[[X-]] columns are ignored by tools that do not
explicitly support them.

We use custom event types [[X-Run.Output]] and
[[X-Run.Input]] rather than embedding all I/O in the
[[Run.Program]] row because interactive programs interleave
output and input---a single column cannot represent the
conversational structure (prompt, response, more output,
etc.).
Timestamps are left empty because learnlog does not record
per-line timing; only the run-level timestamps are
available.

<<emit I/O events for run>>=
io_chunks = chunk_io_log(run["io_log"])
for evt_type, text in io_chunks:
    order += 1
    io_event = {
        "EventType": evt_type,
        "EventID": order,
        "Order": order,
        "SubjectID": subject_id,
        "CodeStateID": code_state_id,
        "ParentEventID": run_event_id,
        "ServerTimestamp": "",
        "ClientTimestamp": "",
        "ProgramResult": "",
        "X-EndTime": "",
        "X-ExitCode": "",
        "X-Script": "",
        "X-Arguments": "",
        "X-ExceptionType": "",
        "X-ExceptionMessage": "",
        "ProgramOutput": "",
        "ProgramInput": "",
        "X-Tag": commit_tags,
        "X-CommitHash": commit_hash,
    }
    resource_path = f"Resources/io_{order}.txt"
    if evt_type == "X-Run.Output":
        io_event["ProgramOutput"] = resource_path
    else:
        io_event["ProgramInput"] = resource_path
    resource_files.append((resource_path, text))
    events.append(io_event)
@

\subsection{What a review looked at}
\label{sec:review-events}

The [[X-Review]] event says that a student sat down with their log;
the [[Viewed:]] lines of its trailer (\cref{sec:view-trace}) say what
they actually put on screen and in what order.
That sequence is the research signal---which failure they went back to,
how far back, how many times---so it must survive into the dataset
rather than being summarised into a count.
A sequence does not fit in a cell, which is the same reason the
captured I/O became child events: we emit one [[X-Review.View]] event
per line of the trace, in trace order, each pointing at its
[[X-Review]] parent exactly as an I/O chunk points at its run.

Two columns carry what is new.
[[X-ViewedCommit]] is the full commit hash the trace recorded, which is
what makes the trace a \emph{join} rather than a note: it matches the
[[X-CommitHash]] of the events generated from that commit, so a query
can go straight from \enquote{the student looked here} to the run and
the code state they were looking at.
[[X-ViewAction]] is the word the viewer chose from its fixed
vocabulary---[[open]], [[next]], [[prev]], [[jump-first]],
[[jump-last]], [[batch]]---and it is what separates a deliberate jump
to the beginning from paging past a commit on the way to somewhere
else.

The timestamps are the trace's own rather than the run's.
The I/O children leave theirs empty because learnlog has no per-line
timing to put there, but a view event has exactly that: the moment the
commit appeared.
Without it the trace would only record an order, and dwell time---how
long a student stayed on a failure before moving on---is measured
between consecutive views, not between runs.

One caveat belongs to the hashes rather than to this code.
When the review was [[learnlog play -f]] on a bundle or a dataset, the
commits on screen were the temporary reconstruction's
(\cref{sec:reconstruct}), so the recorded hashes join within that
session and against the source named in [[X-Arguments]], not against
the student's own log.

<<emit X-Review.View events>>=
for viewed_commit, viewed_at, action in run["viewed"]:
    order += 1
    events.append({
        "EventType": "X-Review.View",
        "EventID": order,
        "Order": order,
        "SubjectID": subject_id,
        "CodeStateID": code_state_id,
        "ParentEventID": run_event_id,
        "ServerTimestamp": viewed_at,
        "ClientTimestamp": viewed_at,
        "ProgramResult": "",
        "X-EndTime": "",
        "X-ExitCode": "",
        "X-Script": "",
        "X-Arguments": "",
        "X-ExceptionType": "",
        "X-ExceptionMessage": "",
        "ProgramOutput": "",
        "ProgramInput": "",
        "X-Tag": commit_tags,
        "X-CommitHash": commit_hash,
        "X-ViewedCommit": viewed_commit,
        "X-ViewAction": action,
    })
@

The branch also declines to export a review run's own captured output,
which is a decision rather than an oversight.
Normally there is none: the recorder installs no I/O wrappers for these
invocations precisely because a batch playback \emph{prints the log},
and capturing that would file a copy of the history inside the history
(\cref{sec:review-runs}).
A log written before that rule, or one where [[play]] output reached
the trailer by some other route, would still carry one---and exporting
it would put every earlier run's output into [[Resources/]] a second
time, attached to an event that did not produce it.
Keeping the I/O branch for program runs only is what keeps
[[Resources/]] a record of what programs printed.

\subsection{Writing the zip file}

We build the zip in a temporary directory, extracting code
states into numbered subdirectories.
The [[DatasetMetadata.csv]] and [[MainTable.csv]] are written
as in-memory strings and added to the zip directly.

<<write progsnap2 zip file>>=
with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(
    prefix="learnlog-ps2-"
) as tmpdir:
    tmppath = pathlib.Path(tmpdir)

    <<extract code states to temp directory>>
    <<build metadata csv string>>
    <<build main table csv string>>
    <<assemble zip file>>
@

We need a reverse mapping from tree hashes to commit hashes so
we can call [[extract_code_state]] with a concrete commit.
Multiple commits may share the same tree hash; we only need one
representative commit per tree, so we keep the first one seen.

<<extract code states to temp directory>>=
states_dir = tmppath / "CodeStates"
states_dir.mkdir()
tree_to_commit = {}
for commit in commits:
    tree_result = repo.git(
        "rev-parse", f"{commit['hash']}^{{tree}}"
    )
    tree_hash = tree_result.stdout.strip()
    if tree_hash not in tree_to_commit:
        tree_to_commit[tree_hash] = commit["hash"]

for tree_hash, state_id in seen_trees.items():
    commit_hash = tree_to_commit[tree_hash]
    state_subdir = states_dir / str(state_id)
    try:
        extract_code_state(repo, commit_hash, state_subdir)
    except GitError as error:
        raise SystemExit(
            f"Failed to extract code state for "
            f"{commit_hash}: {error}"
        ) from error
@

The exporter is where a failed extraction turns from an exception into
a sentence.
[[extract_code_state]] raises [[GitError]] because that is what a
library function owes its callers---the tests below distinguish it, and
so could any future caller---but every path that reaches this chunk
comes from a command a person typed, so it ends the same way
[[do_export]] ends a failed bundle (\cref{sec:export}): one line naming
what could not be read, and a non-zero exit status, rather than a
traceback through a program the user did not write.
The commit hash is in the message because a dataset is exported from a
history the user still has: it is the one piece of information that
makes the failure investigable with [[learnlog git]].

The metadata CSV has two columns: [[Property]] and [[Value]].
We declare version~8, consistent event ordering scoped per
subject, and directory-based code state representation.

<<build metadata csv string>>=
metadata_buf = io.StringIO()
meta_writer = csv.writer(metadata_buf)
meta_writer.writerow(["Property", "Value"])
meta_writer.writerow(["Version", PROGSNAP2_VERSION])
meta_writer.writerow([
    "IsEventOrderingConsistent", "true"
])
meta_writer.writerow([
    "EventOrderScope", "Restricted"
])
meta_writer.writerow([
    "EventOrderScopeColumns", "SubjectID"
])
meta_writer.writerow([
    "CodeStateRepresentation", "Directory"
])
metadata_csv = metadata_buf.getvalue()
@

The main table includes the standard ProgSnap2 columns,
[[ParentEventID]] for linking I/O child events to their
parent [[Run.Program]] (v8, \S4.3.6, lines~902--912),
the standard [[ProgramOutput]] and [[ProgramInput]] columns
referencing files in [[Resources/]], and custom [[X-]]
columns for Python-specific metadata.
The [[X-Tag]] column carries any Git tags that point at
the commit that produced the event, semicolon-delimited
when a commit has multiple tags.
This lets analysis tools segment the dataset by lab session
or other user-defined milestones.
The [[X-CommitHash]] column records the full Git commit hash
that produced each event, enabling tools to filter events by
commit range even after the dataset has been exported to a
zip file.
The optional [[X-EndTime]] column preserves learnlog's run trailer end
time without changing the meaning of [[ServerTimestamp]] and
[[ClientTimestamp]].
The [[X-ViewedCommit]] and [[X-ViewAction]] columns carry the view
trace of a review run (\cref{sec:review-events}); only
[[X-Review.View]] events fill them.
Older datasets remain valid because the reader keeps whatever columns
the CSV provides.

The column list is the union of what every event type needs, and the
writer fills the gaps: [[csv.DictWriter]] substitutes its [[restval]],
the empty string, for any column an event dict omits.
That is what lets each event above list only the columns it actually
has something to say about---a [[File.Edit]] carries no [[X-EndTime]],
a [[Run.Program]] no [[X-ViewedCommit]]---without every new column
having to be threaded back through every other event type.

<<build main table csv string>>=
main_columns = [
    "EventType", "EventID", "Order",
    "SubjectID", "CodeStateID",
    "ParentEventID",
    "ServerTimestamp", "ClientTimestamp",
    "ProgramResult",
    "X-EndTime",
    "X-ExitCode", "X-Script", "X-Arguments",
    "X-ExceptionType", "X-ExceptionMessage",
    "ProgramOutput",
    "ProgramInput",
    "X-Tag",
    "X-CommitHash",
    "X-ViewedCommit",
    "X-ViewAction",
]
main_buf = io.StringIO()
main_writer = csv.DictWriter(
    main_buf, fieldnames=main_columns
)
main_writer.writeheader()
for event in events:
    main_writer.writerow(event)
main_csv = main_buf.getvalue()
@

We write CSVs from in-memory strings rather than temporary files
to avoid extra I/O, and iterate subdirectories in sorted order
so that repeated exports of the same data produce
byte-identical archives---useful for checksumming and caching.
Resource files are likewise written from in-memory strings
collected during event generation; they are already ordered
by [[EventID]], so no additional sorting is needed.

<<assemble zip file>>=
with zipfile.ZipFile(
    str(output_path), "w",
    compression=zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED,
) as zf:
    zf.writestr("DatasetMetadata.csv", metadata_csv)
    zf.writestr("MainTable.csv", main_csv)

    for state_subdir in sorted(states_dir.iterdir()):
        for fpath in sorted(state_subdir.rglob("*")):
            if fpath.is_file():
                arcname = (
                    "CodeStates/"
                    + state_subdir.name
                    + "/"
                    + str(
                        fpath.relative_to(state_subdir)
                    )
                )
                zf.write(str(fpath), arcname)

    for arcname, text in resource_files:
        zf.writestr(arcname, text)
@

Let's verify the complete export pipeline:

<<progsnap2 test functions>>=
def test_do_export_progsnap2_creates_zip(workdir):
    from learnlog.progsnap2 import do_export_progsnap2
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    t1 = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0)
    repo.begin_run("hello.py", [], t1)
    repo.finalize_run(
        io_log="stdout: hello\n",
        exception_info=None,
        end_time=datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, 5),
        exit_code=0,
    )
    output = workdir / "test.progsnap2"
    do_export_progsnap2(repo, output)
    assert output.exists()
    with zipfile.ZipFile(str(output)) as zf:
        names = zf.namelist()
        assert "DatasetMetadata.csv" in names
        assert "MainTable.csv" in names
        code_states = [
            n for n in names
            if n.startswith("CodeStates/")
        ]
        assert len(code_states) >= 1
        resources = [
            n for n in names
            if n.startswith("Resources/")
        ]
        assert len(resources) >= 1
@

An export that cannot read one of its code states must fail as a
message, not as a zip file missing a directory nobody looks in.
We make the extraction fail and check that the exporter stops and says
which commit it stopped on:

<<progsnap2 test functions>>=
def test_do_export_progsnap2_reports_failed_extraction(
    workdir, monkeypatch,
):
    from learnlog import progsnap2
    from learnlog.progsnap2 import do_export_progsnap2

    def failing_extract(repo, commit_hash, state_dir):
        raise GitError(
            ("archive", commit_hash), 128,
            "fatal: not a tree object",
        )

    monkeypatch.setattr(
        progsnap2, "extract_code_state", failing_extract
    )
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    t1 = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0)
    repo.begin_run("hello.py", [], t1)
    output = workdir / "test.progsnap2"

    with pytest.raises(SystemExit) as caught:
        do_export_progsnap2(repo, output)

    assert "not a tree object" in str(caught.value)
    assert not output.exists()
@

<<progsnap2 test functions>>=
def test_progsnap2_metadata_version(workdir):
    import csv as csv_mod
    from learnlog.progsnap2 import do_export_progsnap2
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    output = workdir / "test.progsnap2"
    do_export_progsnap2(repo, output)
    with zipfile.ZipFile(str(output)) as zf:
        meta = zf.read(
            "DatasetMetadata.csv"
        ).decode("utf-8")
    reader = csv_mod.DictReader(io.StringIO(meta))
    rows = {r["Property"]: r["Value"] for r in reader}
    assert rows["Version"] == "8"
    assert rows["CodeStateRepresentation"] == "Directory"

def test_progsnap2_main_table_has_events(workdir):
    import csv as csv_mod
    from learnlog.progsnap2 import do_export_progsnap2
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    t1 = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0)
