\section{Reading a ProgSnap2 dataset}
\label{sec:read-progsnap2}

The export pipeline writes ProgSnap2 datasets; the analysis
pipeline needs to read them back.
Rather than coupling the analyser to the internal export
data structures, we read from the standard zip format.
This means the analyser works equally well on datasets
exported by learnlog and on datasets produced by other
tools that follow the ProgSnap2 specification.

The reader returns a dict with four keys:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[metadata]] ] The [[DatasetMetadata.csv]] key--value
  pairs as a dict.
\item[ [[events]] ] The [[MainTable.csv]] rows as a list of
  dicts, preserving the CSV column names as keys.
\item[ [[code_states]] ] A nested dict mapping each
  [[CodeStateID]] to a dict of
  \{filename: file content\} pairs.  Content is [[str]] for
  files that decode as UTF-8 and [[bytes]] for the rest.
\item[ [[resources]] ] A dict mapping resource paths
  (e.g.\ [[Resources/io_3.txt]]) to their text content.
\end{description}

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def read_progsnap2(source):
    """Read a ProgSnap2 dataset from a zip file.

    ``source`` may be a file path (string or ``Path``) or
    a file-like object (e.g. ``BytesIO``).

    Returns a dict with keys ``metadata``, ``events``,
    ``code_states``, and ``resources``.
    """
    with zipfile.ZipFile(source, "r") as zf:
        <<read metadata csv>>
        <<read main table csv>>
        <<read code states from zip>>
        <<read resource files from zip>>

    return {
        "metadata": metadata,
        "events": events,
        "code_states": code_states,
        "resources": resources,
    }
@

The metadata CSV has two columns ([[Property]] and [[Value]]);
we flatten it into a simple dict.

<<read metadata csv>>=
meta_text = zf.read(
    "DatasetMetadata.csv"
).decode("utf-8")
reader = csv.DictReader(io.StringIO(meta_text))
metadata = {
    row["Property"]: row["Value"]
    for row in reader
}
@

The main table is the heart of the dataset---one row per event.
We preserve all columns as strings, matching the CSV
representation.

<<read main table csv>>=
main_text = zf.read(
    "MainTable.csv"
).decode("utf-8")
reader = csv.DictReader(io.StringIO(main_text))
events = list(reader)
@

Code states live under [[CodeStates/{id}/]] in the zip.
A code state is a snapshot of the student's entire working tree, and a
working tree is not necessarily text: a sprite for a game, a compiled
[[a.out]], a [[.pyc]], or a PDF handed in with the code all end up
there because [[extract_code_state]] archives whatever Git tracked.
Reading every entry as UTF-8 therefore cannot work.
Skipping the undecodable ones---what we used to do---is worse than it
sounds: [[restore_code_state]] treats the code state as the
\emph{whole} tree, so a file missing from the dict is a file
\emph{deleted} from the reconstructed working tree, and playback of a
student's own project silently loses their data files.

We therefore keep the decoded [[str]] when the bytes are valid UTF-8
and the raw [[bytes]] when they are not.
The type then carries what we actually know about the file: text for
everything the diff and \LaTeX{} machinery can work with, bytes for
what only a byte-exact round trip can preserve.
This is the same text-versus-binary distinction Git itself makes, and
it keeps the common case---source code---unchanged for every consumer.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def decode_if_text(data):
    """Return ``data`` decoded as UTF-8, or the raw bytes.

    Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 are returned unchanged so that
    binary files survive a round trip byte for byte.
    """
    try:
        return data.decode("utf-8")
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        return data
@

With the decoding decision isolated, the reader itself only has to
split each archive entry into its state ID and its path inside that
state:

<<read code states from zip>>=
code_states = {}
for name in zf.namelist():
    if not name.startswith("CodeStates/"):
        continue
    parts = name[len("CodeStates/"):].split("/", 1)
    if len(parts) < 2 or not parts[1]:
        continue
    state_id = parts[0]
    filename = parts[1]
    if state_id not in code_states:
        code_states[state_id] = {}
    code_states[state_id][filename] = decode_if_text(
        zf.read(name)
    )
@

Resource files (I/O text) live under [[Resources/]].
Here the same fallback would be useless: a resource is a captured I/O
log whose only destination is a reconstructed commit message, and
there is nothing sensible to do with raw bytes on that path.
Our own exporter writes these from [[str]], so they are always valid
UTF-8, but a dataset produced by another tool---or by a future version
that captures I/O at the file-descriptor level---may not be.
Decoding with [[errors="replace"]] turns that into a few replacement
characters in one line of output instead of a [[UnicodeDecodeError]]
that makes the entire dataset unreadable.

<<read resource files from zip>>=
resources = {}
for name in zf.namelist():
    if not name.startswith("Resources/"):
        continue
    resources[name] = zf.read(name).decode(
        "utf-8", errors="replace"
    )
@

