\section{Reconstructing a temporary repository}
\label{sec:reconstruct}

The [[analyse]] command can work directly from the flat event
stream, but [[play]] and [[list]] already rely on Git commit
queries, diffs, and tag resolution.
Rather than build a second playback stack for ProgSnap2, we
reconstruct a temporary [[LearnlogRepo]] from the dataset and
reuse the existing Git-based code.

The reconstruction groups events by [[X-CommitHash]], restores
each commit's full code snapshot from [[CodeStates/]], rebuilds
the run blocks from the [[Run.Program]] and child I/O events,
and recreates any tags recorded in [[X-Tag]].
Using full snapshots matters because ProgSnap2 stores complete
code states, not incremental patches: if a file disappears from
the next state, we must delete it from the temporary working
tree too.

Two details need explicit care.
First, [[LearnlogRepo(tmpdir / "repo")]] auto-creates an
initial commit; the first reconstructed commit therefore amends
that commit instead of adding an extra synthetic one.
This is the one [[--amend]] left in the program, and it does not
conflict with the append-only rule of
\cref{sec:two-commit-protocol}: that rule protects a \emph{live}
log that overlapping runs, pushes, and tags may be reading while
it grows, whereas this repository is a private scratch copy that
one process builds in one pass and deletes after playback.
Nothing observes its intermediate states, so replacing the
placeholder is safe---and better than keeping it, since a
synthetic root commit would show up as a run that never
happened.
Second, ProgSnap2's I/O model merges [[stderr]] into output and
still cannot recover that distinction.
We therefore rebuild the header faithfully, preserve end time when
[[X-EndTime]] is available, and keep the original exit code,
exception, and merged I/O.

The public entry point returns the reconstructed repository and
the temporary directory that owns it.  The caller removes that
directory after playback or listing finishes.

Everything after [[mkdtemp]] therefore runs inside a guard, for the
reasons spelled out where [[resolve_source]] does the same for
bundles (\cref{sec:resolve-source}): the directory is ours
until we hand it back by returning, and once it becomes ours nothing
but this function knows its name.
Reconstruction is the slowest thing learnlog does---one Git commit
per recorded run, each restoring a full code snapshot---so it is
also the operation a student is most likely to interrupt, and the
one that leaves the largest thing behind when they do: not an empty
directory but a complete repository.
The guard starts at [[mkdtemp]] rather than at the replay loop
because [[LearnlogRepo]] runs [[git init]] and two [[git config]]
calls before the first run is replayed, and a directory abandoned
during setup is just as lost as one abandoned mid-replay.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def read_progsnap2_as_repo(source):
    """Reconstruct a temporary ``LearnlogRepo`` from ProgSnap2.

    Returns ``(repo, tmpdir)`` where ``tmpdir`` is a temporary
    directory path that the caller should remove when done.
    """
    dataset = read_progsnap2(source)
    commit_groups = group_events_by_commit(dataset["events"])
    if not commit_groups:
        raise SystemExit("No commits found in ProgSnap2 dataset.")

    tmpdir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="learnlog-ps2-play-")
    try:
        <<reconstruct the repository under [[tmpdir]]>>
    except BaseException:
        shutil.rmtree(tmpdir, ignore_errors=True)
        raise
@

The [[except Exception]] this replaces caught the reconstruction
failures---a malformed dataset, a snapshot Git refused---and by
construction could not catch [[KeyboardInterrupt]], which is how the
leak was reported in the first place.

<<reconstruct the repository under [[tmpdir]]>>=
repo_path = pathlib.Path(tmpdir) / "repo"
repo_path.mkdir()
repo = LearnlogRepo(repo_path)
subject_id = commit_groups[0]["events"][0].get(
    "SubjectID", "learnlog"
) or "learnlog"

<<configure reconstructed repository identity>>
<<replay grouped commits into temporary repository>>

return repo, tmpdir
@

We preserve the student's identity in the temporary history so
that [[git log]] and any later export still attribute the runs
to the same [[SubjectID]].

<<configure reconstructed repository identity>>=
repo.git("config", "user.name", subject_id)
repo.git(
    "config", "user.email",
    f"{subject_id.lower().replace(' ', '.')}@learnlog.invalid",
)
@

Each commit group restores the corresponding snapshot, stages it,
rebuilds the commit message, and either amends the auto-created
initial commit or creates a new one.  After the commit exists, we
add any tags that were exported for that group.

<<replay grouped commits into temporary repository>>=
for index, group in enumerate(commit_groups):
    restore_code_state(
        repo.workdir,
        dataset["code_states"].get(group["code_state_id"], {}),
    )
    <<stage the restored code state>>

    message = build_reconstructed_commit_message(
        group, dataset["resources"]
    )

    <<write reconstructed commit>>
    <<recreate tags for reconstructed commit>>
@

\subsection{Staging a code state the dataset is the authority on}
\label{sec:reconstruction-staging}

We are committing into a [[LearnlogRepo]], so it carries learnlog's
exclude patterns like any other (\cref{sec:ensure-configured}), and
two of them---[[*.learnlog]] and [[*.progsnap2]]---name the files
[[learnlog export]] writes.
Those patterns are a policy about what a \emph{new} run records: a
bundle sitting in the work tree is the export the log already
contains, and committing it doubles the log on every cycle.
They say nothing about what an existing dataset reports, and on this
side the dataset is the authority.
Everything in a code state was, by construction, tracked in the log it
came from---[[git archive]] exports a committed tree
(\cref{sec:extract-code-state})---so a dataset exported before those
patterns existed can legitimately carry a file with either suffix.
Letting the exclude file drop it would make the reconstructed history
disagree with the dataset it was rebuilt from, and disagree silently,
because a file Git declines to add is not an error.
Hence [[-f]]: the restore decides what is in the tree, and staging
takes all of it.

That leaves the one pattern which is not policy.
[[.learnlog]] is the Git directory of the very repository we are
committing into, and it sits inside the work tree, so it is an
ordinary directory to [[git add]] and is kept out of its own history
by that pattern and nothing else (\cref{sec:ensure-configured}).
A bare [[git add -f .]] would stage [[HEAD]], the index, the object
store and the lock file as project files.
Naming it as an excluded pathspec is what makes [[-f]] override the
policy patterns without touching the structural one.
[[safe_snapshot_path]] already refuses to \emph{write} anything under
that directory, so the pathspec is the second half of a rule the
restore states first: nothing the dataset names can land there, and
nothing learnlog itself put there can be staged out of it.

<<stage the restored code state>>=
repo.git(
    "add", "-f", "--", ".", GIT_DIR_PATHSPEC,
    timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
)
<<progsnap2 constants>>=
GIT_DIR_PATHSPEC = f":(exclude){GIT_DIR_NAME}"
@

Building the pathspec from [[GIT_DIR_NAME]] rather than spelling
[[.learnlog]] again is the same reasoning that names the exclude
pattern from it: the directory and the one thing protecting it should
not be able to disagree.

The first group replaces the placeholder initial commit that
[[LearnlogRepo]] created during setup.  Later groups become
ordinary commits.

A reconstructed message contains the same captured I/O as the message
it was exported from, so it is subject to the same argument-size limit
(\cref{sec:commit-message-stdin}) and travels the same way: on standard
input.
Without that, importing a dataset would silently drop exactly the runs
that were interesting enough to produce a lot of output.

<<write reconstructed commit>>=
if index == 0:
    repo.git(
        "commit", "--amend", "--allow-empty",
        "--reset-author",
        "--file=-", input=message,
        timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
    )
else:
    repo.git(
        "commit", "--allow-empty",
        "--file=-", input=message,
        timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
    )
@

Tag recreation must happen after the commit is written because
Git tags point at concrete commit objects.  The exporter stores
multiple tags as a semicolon-delimited [[X-Tag]] value, so we
reuse [[_iter_event_tags]] to split them consistently.

<<recreate tags for reconstructed commit>>=
for tag in _iter_event_tags(group["tags"]):
    if tag:
        repo.git("tag", tag)
@

We group events by [[X-CommitHash]] in their event order.
Each group keeps the commit hash only as a stable key while we
replay; the reconstructed Git repository will naturally assign
new commit hashes.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def group_events_by_commit(events):
    """Group ProgSnap2 events by their original commit hash."""
    by_commit = {}
    for event in sorted(
        events, key=lambda e: int(e["Order"])
    ):
        commit_hash = event.get("X-CommitHash", "")
        if not commit_hash:
            continue
        if commit_hash not in by_commit:
            by_commit[commit_hash] = {
                "hash": commit_hash,
                "events": [],
                "code_state_id": event.get(
                    "CodeStateID", ""
                ),
                "tags": event.get("X-Tag", "") or "",
            }
        group = by_commit[commit_hash]
        group["events"].append(event)
        if event.get("CodeStateID"):
            group["code_state_id"] = event[
                "CodeStateID"
            ]
        if event.get("X-Tag"):
            group["tags"] = event["X-Tag"]
    return sorted(
        by_commit.values(),
        key=lambda group: int(group["events"][0]["Order"]),
    )
@

\begin{description}
\item[Snapshots] We treat the recorded code state as the whole
  tree.  Files present in the working tree but absent from the
  new snapshot are removed.
\item[Directories] We create parent directories on demand so
  nested files under [[CodeStates/]] restore cleanly.
\item[Log repository] The reconstructed repository keeps its Git
  directory at [[.learnlog]] \emph{inside} the working tree it is
  rebuilding, so a snapshot must never touch that path.
\item[File types] Content is written back with the same type it was
  read as, so binary files round-trip unchanged.
\item[Untrusted names] Every entry name is checked before it is
  joined onto the working directory, because a name in a dataset is
  not necessarily a name that stays inside one.
\end{description}

The [[.learnlog]] rule is the one that was previously only half
applied.
Skipping the directory while deleting but not while writing looks
harmless, because learnlog's own exporter never puts [[.learnlog]]
paths into a code state.
It is not: a code state is student-controlled data, so a project that
happens to contain a directory called [[.learnlog]]---or a dataset
from another tool, or a hand-edited one---makes the write loop
overwrite [[HEAD]], the index, or a ref of the very repository we are
committing into, mid-replay.
Expressing both passes through one predicate is what makes the guard
symmetric by construction rather than by inspection.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def is_learnlog_path(name):
    """Return whether ``name`` lies inside the ``.learnlog`` directory."""
    parts = pathlib.PurePath(name).parts
    return bool(parts) and parts[0] == ".learnlog"
@

[[.learnlog]] was only the nearest instance of a larger problem.
A code-state entry is a \emph{name} that we join onto [[workdir]], and
the names come from the zip index of a dataset learnlog did not
necessarily write: a hand-edited file, an export from another tool, a
classmate's dataset arriving by mail.
Two shapes of name that [[pathlib]] accepts do not stay under
[[workdir]] at all.
A name like [[../id_rsa]] climbs out of the directory, and an absolute
one discards it entirely---[[workdir / "/etc/passwd"]] \emph{is}
[[/etc/passwd]], because joining an absolute path replaces the left
operand instead of extending it.
Playing back an untrusted dataset would then write attacker-chosen
content wherever the student can write.

The decision is one function, applied to every entry before anything
is created, and it hands back the path to write rather than a yes or
no: the join is the very thing being judged, so returning it is what
stops the caller from computing it a second time and differently.
It answers in two halves---a name half, which we can settle without
touching the disk, and a filesystem half, which we cannot---and
neither half subsumes the other.

The name half rejects what cannot be a plain relative path: an empty
name or [[.]], which select no file; a root or a drive, which make the
join absolute; and any [[..]] component, which walks upwards.
We read the name as a [[PureWindowsPath]] even on Unix.
That is not portability decoration.
[[PureWindowsPath]] treats both slashes as separators and recognises
drive letters and UNC prefixes, so it sees [[C:/secrets]] and
[[a\..\..\x]] as the paths whoever wrote them meant.
A dataset is a file that travels between machines, and a check that
only understands the conventions of the machine it happens to run on
is a check an attacker satisfies by choosing the other platform's
spelling.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def is_safe_relative_name(name):
    """Return whether ``name`` is a plain relative path.

    Rejects absolute names, drive and UNC prefixes, ``..``
    components, and names that select no file at all.  The name is
    read with Windows separator rules so that a dataset written on
    either platform is judged the same way.
    """
    pure = pathlib.PureWindowsPath(name)
    if pure.drive or pure.root:
        return False
    parts = pure.parts
    return bool(parts) and ".." not in parts
@

The filesystem half answers the question a name cannot: where does the
join actually land?
A name can be relative, [[..]]-free and still escape through a
symbolic link that already exists in the tree---with [[data]] pointing
at [[/tmp]], the entry [[data/x]] lands in [[/tmp/x]].
Resolving the join and requiring the result to stay under the resolved
[[workdir]] settles that, because resolution is exactly what follows
those links.
We resolve [[workdir]] as well: comparing a resolved path against an
unresolved root would report an escape for every project that lives
under a symlinked home directory.
Resolution can still refuse, on a symlink loop; refusing to write is
then the answer we already wanted.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def safe_snapshot_path(workdir, name):
    """Return where ``name`` may be restored, relative to ``workdir``.

    Returns ``None`` when the entry must not be written: when it is
    not a plain relative path, when it reaches into the ``.learnlog``
    directory of the repository being rebuilt, or when the join
    leaves ``workdir`` through a symbolic link.
    """
    if not is_safe_relative_name(name):
        return None
    parts = pathlib.PureWindowsPath(name).parts
    relative = pathlib.Path(*parts)
    if is_learnlog_path(relative):
        return None
    try:
        target = (workdir / relative).resolve()
        if not target.is_relative_to(workdir.resolve()):
            return None
    except OSError:
        return None
    return relative
@

Rebuilding the relative path from the parsed parts rather than using
the name as given is what makes the [[.learnlog]] check and the join
see the same path the name check saw: a Windows-style
[[.learnlog\HEAD]] is one opaque file name to [[PurePath]] on Unix,
and it must not slip past a guard that has already read it as two
components.

An unsafe entry is skipped, with a line on standard error naming it,
and the rest of the state restores normally.
Refusing the whole dataset is the other defensible answer, and for a
tool that only ever read its own exports it would be the better one.
This one reads foreign datasets on purpose---that is what
[[learnlog play -f]] and [[learnlog analyse]] are for---and a
researcher looking at someone else's data is better served by the
files that were fine plus a visible complaint than by a command that
refuses an entire dataset over one bad name.
What we must not do is skip in silence: the same entry is an attack in
one dataset and a bug in the exporter that produced another, and
neither gets fixed by a tool that says nothing.

The message goes to [[sys.stderr]], not to the [[sys.__stderr__]] that
[[report_error]] uses (\cref{sec:diagnostics}).
The difference is who is meant to read it.
[[report_error]] reports learnlog failing behind a student's back and
must stay out of the run it describes, whereas this is a command
talking to the person who typed it, and it belongs in that command's
output like every other message [[play]] and [[analyse]] print.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def warn_unsafe_snapshot_path(name):
    """Report a code-state entry that was not restored."""
    print(
        f"learnlog: skipping unsafe code state path {name!r}",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
@

Writing the snapshot has to preserve the distinction
[[read_progsnap2]] recorded: [[str]] goes through [[write_text]] and
[[bytes]] through [[write_bytes]].
Forcing everything through [[write_text]] would raise on a binary file,
and forcing everything through [[write_bytes]] would encode text with
the platform's default rules instead of the UTF-8 we decoded from.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def restore_code_state(workdir, code_state):
    """Replace ``workdir`` contents with a full code snapshot."""
    <<vet the snapshot entries>>

    for path in sorted(workdir.rglob("*"), reverse=True):
        rel = path.relative_to(workdir)
        if is_learnlog_path(rel):
            continue
        if path.is_symlink():
            path.unlink()
        elif path.is_dir():
            if any(rel in item.parents for item in expected):
                continue
            path.rmdir()
        elif rel not in expected:
            path.unlink()

    for relative, content in snapshot.items():
        target = workdir / relative
        target.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        if isinstance(content, bytes):
            target.write_bytes(content)
        else:
            target.write_text(content)
@

The symbolic-link branch of the deletion pass exists because [[Path]]
answers the question above it the wrong way for us.
[[rglob]] yields links alongside real entries, and [[is_dir]] follows
them, so a link pointing at a directory says yes---and [[rmdir]] then
refuses that same link with [[NotADirectoryError]], because the link
is not a directory, it only points at one.
Removing a link is [[unlink]] whatever it points at, and that is also
the only removal that leaves the far end alone: [[rmdir]] here would
have been a request to delete somebody else's directory.

We remove links without asking whether the snapshot claims the name.
A code state records file contents, so restoring an entry means
creating a file under that name, whereas a link left in place would
make the write pass follow it and overwrite whatever is at the other
end.
That is the substitution [[safe_snapshot_path]] already refuses when
it judges a name; arriving at it from the tree instead of from the
name does not make it a different thing, and deleting the link first
is what makes the two passes agree---the name is created, not
followed.

No caller reaches this today.
[[restore_code_state]] rebuilds into a temporary tree that playback
creates for it (\cref{sec:reconstruct}), and a code state carries file
contents rather than links, so nothing puts a symbolic link in the
tree being walked.
The branch is here because the loop is written against the filesystem
and not against that single caller: a restore over a directory that
already existed, or a dataset that does carry a link, turns a missing
predicate into an exception raised halfway through deleting, leaving a
tree that is neither the old one nor the new one.
A pass that empties a directory has to be one that finishes.

Vetting happens before either pass, and both passes then work from its
result.
That ordering is what keeps the two halves consistent: a rejected entry
is not written, and---because the deletion pass removes whatever the
snapshot does not claim---it is also not treated as a file the
reconstructed tree ought to contain.

<<vet the snapshot entries>>=
snapshot = {}
for name, content in code_state.items():
    relative = safe_snapshot_path(workdir, name)
    if relative is None:
        warn_unsafe_snapshot_path(name)
        continue
    snapshot[relative] = content
expected = set(snapshot)
@

Rebuilding the commit message mirrors the original structure as
closely as the dataset allows.
We order events by [[Order]], start each run block from the
stored script, arguments, and start time, then reconstruct the
I/O and exception sections from the child events and resource
files.

Datasets exported by current learnlog versions also carry
[[X-EndTime]], so reconstruction can rebuild the original trailer.
Older datasets remain readable because a missing [[X-EndTime]] simply
means the rebuilt block omits the [[End-Time:]] line.

The message is rebuilt from the events that \emph{are} runs, and there
are two kinds: the student's programs and their reviews.
Both become the same kind of block---a review run is written to the log
by the same [[begin_run]] and [[finalize_run]] as any other
(\cref{sec:review-runs}), so reconstructing it is not a second code
path but the same one applied to a second event type.
What differs is only which children the block draws on: a
[[Run.Program]] has I/O chunks and no view trace, an [[X-Review]] the
reverse.
Asking for both and letting the empty one come back empty is what makes
that possible: a branch on the event type would have to be right about
which kind of child each type has, and a dataset that disagreed---a
review carrying I/O from an older exporter, say---would lose whatever
the branch did not expect to find.

Selecting by an explicit tuple rather than by exclusion is what keeps
the child events out of the block list.
An [[X-Review.View]] event sits in the group's event list beside its
parent, and a loop that skipped only [[X-Run.Output]] and
[[X-Run.Input]] would try to build a run block out of it.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def build_reconstructed_commit_message(group, resources):
    """Build a learnlog-style commit message from one group."""
    blocks = []
    events = sorted(
        group["events"],
        key=lambda event: int(event["Order"]),
    )

    for event in events:
        if event["EventType"] not in RECONSTRUCTED_RUN_EVENTS:
            continue
        io_log = rebuild_io_log(
            group["events"], event["EventID"], resources
        )
        view_trace = rebuild_view_trace(
            group["events"], event["EventID"]
        )
        blocks.append(
            build_reconstructed_run_block(
                event, io_log, view_trace
            )
        )

    if not blocks:
        return "learnlog: reconstructed commit"

    return BLOCK_SEP.join(blocks)
<<progsnap2 constants>>=
RECONSTRUCTED_RUN_EVENTS = ("Run.Program", "X-Review")
@

We reuse [[format_run_header]] so the first half of each block is
identical to a native learnlog commit.
When [[X-EndTime]] is present we also reuse [[format_run_trailer]],
which keeps the reconstructed trailer aligned with a native learnlog
run apart from the already-lost [[stderr]] split.
For older datasets without [[X-EndTime]], we fall back to the older
behaviour and omit the [[End-Time:]] line.

[[X-Arguments]] holds the field exactly as the header wrote it, so
recovering the list is [[parse_run_arguments]] and nothing else
(\cref{sec:run-arguments}).
Splitting the column on whitespace instead---which is what this did
before---turns one argument containing a space into two, and a
[[python -c]] source into as many arguments as it has words, so the
block we rebuild would not be the block we exported.

A review run's [[Viewed:]] lines come back the same way its I/O would:
handed to [[format_run_trailer]], which is the one place that knows
where in the trailer they go (\cref{sec:finalising-a-run}).
Reusing the writer rather than assembling the lines here is what makes
the round trip exact instead of approximately right---a reconstructed
trailer is byte-identical to the one the run wrote, and stays so if the
layout ever changes.

The fallback for datasets without [[X-EndTime]] therefore needs no view
trace of its own.
[[End-Time:]] and the [[Viewed:]] lines are written by the same
[[finalize_run]] into the same trailer, so a dataset that lost the one
never carried the other; anything that did would have to be a hand-made
file, and the header we still emit for it is the honest reconstruction
of what it records.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def build_reconstructed_run_block(event, io_log, view_trace=None):
    """Rebuild one run block from a ProgSnap2 run event."""
    script = event.get("X-Script", "") or "(unknown)"
    args = parse_run_arguments(event.get("X-Arguments", ""))
    start_time = datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(
        event["ServerTimestamp"]
    )
    header = format_run_header(script, args, start_time)
    exception_info = rebuild_exception_info(event)
    exit_code = event.get("X-ExitCode", "")
    end_time_value = event.get("X-EndTime", "")

    if not exit_code and not io_log and not exception_info:
        return header

    if end_time_value:
        end_time = datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(end_time_value)
        trailer = format_run_trailer(
            io_log,
            exception_info,
            end_time,
            int(exit_code) if exit_code else 0,
            view_trace,
        )
        return f"{header}\n{trailer}"

    parts = [header]
    if exit_code:
        parts.append(f"Exit-Code: {exit_code}")
    if io_log or exception_info:
        parts.extend([
            "",
            "--- I/O ---",
            io_log.rstrip("\n") if io_log else "(no I/O)",
            "",
            "--- exception ---",
            exception_info or "None",
        ])
    return "\n".join(parts)
@

The exception text stored in ProgSnap2 is split into type and
message columns.
For playback we only need a readable final line, so we join them
back into the conventional [[Type: message]] form when both are
present.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def rebuild_exception_info(event):
    """Reconstruct readable exception text from event columns."""
    exc_type = event.get("X-ExceptionType", "")
    exc_msg = event.get("X-ExceptionMessage", "")
    if exc_type and exc_msg:
        return f"{exc_type}: {exc_msg}"
    return exc_type or exc_msg or None
@

ProgSnap2 stores child I/O events separately from the parent run.
We rebuild learnlog's textual I/O log by walking the matching
child events in order and reattaching the original [[stdout:]]/
[[stdin:]] prefixes.
The [[stderr]] distinction is already lost at export time, so all
output is reconstructed as [[stdout:]].

Here we must preserve learnlog's line-oriented encoding exactly.
The exporter records a blank output line as an empty output entry,
which becomes a trailing newline in the resource text.
Reconstruction therefore has to use [[split("\n")]] rather than
[[splitlines()]]: the trailing empty element is not a stray artifact,
but the representation of the student's actual blank line.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def rebuild_io_log(events, parent_event_id, resources):
    """Rebuild learnlog's line-oriented I/O log for one run."""
    lines = []
    children = sorted(
        [
            event for event in events
            if event.get("ParentEventID") == parent_event_id
        ],
        key=lambda event: int(event["Order"]),
    )
    for event in children:
        if event["EventType"] == "X-Run.Output":
            resource = event.get("ProgramOutput", "")
            prefix = "stdout: "
        elif event["EventType"] == "X-Run.Input":
            resource = event.get("ProgramInput", "")
            prefix = "stdin: "
        else:
            continue
        text = resources.get(resource, "")
        lines.extend(
            f"{prefix}{line}" for line in text.split("\n")
        )
    return "\n".join(lines).rstrip("\n")
@

The [[else: continue]] above is what makes the two child kinds
independent: a run's children are whatever events name it as parent,
and the loop reads only the ones it recognises.
An [[X-Review.View]] among them is therefore not a case to handle but a
case already handled, which is the same tolerance the pairing loop
(\cref{sec:group-pairs}) relies on.

The view trace comes back by the mirror-image walk.
Where [[rebuild_io_log]] returns text, this returns the triples
[[format_run_trailer]] expects---the timestamp first, because that is
the order [[learnlog.record_view]] stores them in and the order the
writer's parameters take (\cref{gitrepo}), even though the line itself
leads with the commit.

A view whose timestamp will not parse is dropped rather than
substituted for, because the substitute would be a lie: an invented
time in a trace read as a chronology is worse than a shorter trace, and
the trace is the only thing in the dataset whose timestamps are read as
the events they describe rather than as metadata about a run.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def rebuild_view_trace(events, parent_event_id):
    """Rebuild a review run's view trace from its child events.

    Returns ``(time, commit, action)`` triples in event order,
    ready for :func:`learnlog.gitrepo.format_run_trailer`.
    Views with an unreadable timestamp are skipped.
    """
    trace = []
    children = sorted(
        [
            event for event in events
            if event.get("ParentEventID") == parent_event_id
            and event["EventType"] == "X-Review.View"
        ],
        key=lambda event: int(event["Order"]),
    )
    for event in children:
        viewed_at = _parse_iso(event.get("ServerTimestamp"))
        if viewed_at is None:
            continue
        trace.append((
            viewed_at,
            event.get("X-ViewedCommit", ""),
            event.get("X-ViewAction", ""),
        ))
    return trace
@

