An absolute name is the same attack without the [[..]], and it is the
one [[pathlib]] makes easiest to write by accident: the join simply
discards the working directory.
Here the target is a real file that exists, so the test can assert the
stronger property---that its contents are untouched:

<<progsnap2 test functions>>=
def test_restore_code_state_refuses_absolute_names(
    tmp_path, capsys,
):
    from learnlog.progsnap2 import restore_code_state

    workdir = tmp_path / "work"
    workdir.mkdir()
    victim = tmp_path / "victim.py"
    victim.write_text("original\n")

    restore_code_state(workdir, {
