\section{\LaTeX{} analysis output}
\label{sec:latex-analysis}

The analysis generates \LaTeX{} source that presents
the student's edit--run cycle.
The output uses [[minted]] for syntax-highlighted code
listings and [[paracol]] for the side-by-side mode.
We generate a fragment by default (sections and
subsections that the user [[\\input]]s into their own
document) and optionally a standalone document.


\subsection{Computing run gaps within a tag}

The replay view only knows commit dates, but the analysis report already has
the run's start and end timestamps.
That lets us measure the more precise interval that matters pedagogically:
the time from the \emph{end} of the previous run to the \emph{start} of the
current one.

We compute this over the already grouped [[pairs]] list for one
[[SubjectID]] and one tag.
That means the timer resets automatically at tag boundaries, which matches the
intended interpretation of tags as session breaks rather than ordinary pauses
inside one working session.

If a previous run has no recorded end time, we fall back to its start time.
That preserves a useful estimate for interrupted runs without pretending we
know more than the data records.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def compute_run_gaps(pairs):
    """Return per-run end-to-start gaps for one pair list.

    The returned dict maps each run ``EventID`` to seconds
    since the previous run in ``pairs``.  The first run maps
    to ``None``.  Edit-only pairs are skipped.
    """
    gaps = {}
    prev_end = None

    for pair in pairs:
        run = pair.get("run")
        if run is None:
            continue

        start = _parse_iso(run.get("ServerTimestamp"))
        if start is not None and prev_end is not None:
            gap = (start - prev_end).total_seconds()
        else:
            gap = None
        gaps[run["EventID"]] = gap

        prev_end = _parse_iso(run.get("X-EndTime")) or start

    return gaps
@

