\subsection{Formatting a side-by-side row}

Each row of the causal chain is a pair of
top-aligned [[minipage]] boxes separated by
[[\hfill]].
The [[[t]]] alignment flag is what guarantees row
alignment: both boxes line up by their first line,
so the two [[\paragraph]] headings sit on the same
baseline regardless of whether one half contains a
two-line diff and the other half a twenty-line run.
Without [[[t]]], LaTeX centres the boxes on each
other's midlines and the headings end up at
different vertical positions.

[[\noindent]] is needed because we emit the row at
paragraph level: without it, the left [[minipage]]
would be shifted right by [[\parindent]] and the
row would lose its symmetry with neighbouring rows.
[[\hfill]] between the boxes absorbs whatever
horizontal space is left over so the right
[[minipage]] reaches the right margin exactly.
A small [[\vspace]] below the row separates it from
the next one---without it, two consecutive rows
would run together and obscure the chain structure.

We keep the cell construction as a nested helper so
that the left and right halves are emitted by the
same code path and an empty half cannot accidentally
omit its [[minipage]] wrapper (which would collapse
the row and misalign the chain).

The [[fullwidth]] flag controls whether the row sits
at the surrounding body text width or breaks out to
the full page width via the [[fullwidth]] environment
from the [[didactic]] package.  Without it, two
[[0.48\linewidth]] minipages share the article body's
text block---about 12--13\,cm on A4---which is too
narrow for the typical diff-plus-stdout pair: long
identifiers wrap mid-token and tracebacks lose their
column alignment.  Wrapping just the paired rows
keeps the surrounding prose (variation-theory notes,
section headings) at the comfortable body width while
giving the code its breathing room.  Callers that
render the first edit or an unpaired run leave
[[fullwidth]] at [[False]], so unpaired content stays
in the normal text column.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def format_minipage_row(
    left_label, left_tex,
    right_label, right_tex,
    width="0.48\\linewidth",
    fullwidth=False,
):
    """Return LaTeX lines for one side-by-side row.

    The row is a pair of top-aligned ``minipage``
    boxes separated by ``\\hfill``.  An empty half
    still renders as an empty ``minipage`` so the
    surrounding rows stay aligned.

    When ``fullwidth`` is true, the row is wrapped in
    a ``fullwidth`` environment (from the ``didactic``
    package) so the paired minipages span the full
    page width instead of the article body width.
    """
    def cell(label, tex):
        out = [
            f"\\begin{{minipage}}[t]{{{width}}}"
        ]
        if tex:
            out.append(f"\\paragraph{{{label}}}")
            out.append(tex)
        out.append("\\end{minipage}")
        return out

    row = [
        "\\noindent",
        *cell(left_label, left_tex),
        "\\hfill",
        *cell(right_label, right_tex),
    ]
    if fullwidth:
        row = (
            ["\\begin{fullwidth}"]
            + row
            + ["\\end{fullwidth}"]
        )
    return row + ["", "\\vspace{1em}", ""]
@

\subsection{Formatting an edit}

An edit is shown as a unified diff between the previous
and current code states.
For the first edit (no previous state), we show the full
file content instead---the student wrote this code from
scratch.
If the pair has no edit ([[edit=None]], a re-run), we
return an empty string.

We use [[difflib.unified_diff]] to compute the diff.
When multiple files changed, we concatenate the diffs
for each file.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def format_edit_latex(pair, prev_pair, code_states):
    """Format the edit part of a pair as LaTeX.

    Returns a string with a ``minted`` block showing
    either a unified diff or the full file content.
    Returns an empty string if there is no edit.
    """
    if pair["edit"] is None:
        return ""

    state_id = pair["code_state_id"]
    current = code_states.get(state_id, {})

    prev_state_id = (
        prev_pair["code_state_id"]
        if prev_pair is not None
        else None
    )
    previous = (
        code_states.get(prev_state_id, {})
        if prev_state_id is not None
        else {}
    )

    if not previous:
        <<format first edit as full file>>
    else:
        <<format edit as unified diff>>
@

Now that code states keep binary files instead of dropping them
(\cref{sec:read-progsnap2}), the formatter has to say something about
a file it cannot typeset.
Neither [[minted]] nor [[difflib]] can do anything useful with a PNG,
so we report its presence and size and move on; silently omitting it
would make the report claim the student's tree contained only the
text files.
The note ends with a newline so that joining the parts leaves a blank
line after it; two binary files in a row would otherwise run together
into a single \LaTeX{} paragraph.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def format_binary_file_latex(filename, content):
    """Note a binary code-state file that cannot be shown."""
    return (
        "\\textbf{Binary file: "
        f"\\texttt{{{_latex_escape(filename)}}}"
        f"}} ({len(content)} bytes, content not shown)\n"
    )
@

When there is no previous state, we show each file
in its entirety.
To keep the filename visible in the rendered document, we emit
an explicit label rather than a LaTeX comment.

<<format first edit as full file>>=
parts = []
for filename in sorted(current.keys()):
    content = current[filename]
    if isinstance(content, bytes):
        parts.append(
            format_binary_file_latex(filename, content)
        )
        continue
    parts.append(
        "\\textbf{File: "
        f"\\texttt{{{_latex_escape(filename)}}}"
        "}"
    )
    parts.append("")
    parts.append("\\begin{minted}{python}")
    parts.append(content.rstrip())
    parts.append("\\end{minted}")
return "\n".join(parts)
@

For subsequent edits, we compute a unified diff per
file.
Files that did not change are skipped.
New files show as additions; deleted files as removals.
A file that is binary on either side gets the same note as above
rather than a diff: comparing the two byte strings is enough to tell
that it changed, which is all a diff of unprintable data could
convey anyway.

<<format edit as unified diff>>=
all_files = sorted(
    set(list(previous.keys())
        + list(current.keys()))
)
parts = []
for filename in all_files:
    old = previous.get(filename, "")
    new = current.get(filename, "")
    if old == new:
        continue
    if isinstance(new, bytes) or isinstance(old, bytes):
        changed = new if isinstance(new, bytes) else old
        parts.append(
            format_binary_file_latex(filename, changed)
        )
        continue
    diff_lines = list(difflib.unified_diff(
        old.splitlines(keepends=True),
        new.splitlines(keepends=True),
        fromfile=f"a/{filename}",
        tofile=f"b/{filename}",
    ))
    if diff_lines:
        parts.append("\\begin{minted}{diff}")
        parts.append(
            "".join(diff_lines).rstrip()
        )
        parts.append("\\end{minted}")
if not parts:
    return ""
return "\n".join(parts)
@

\subsection{Formatting a run}

A run shows the script name, result classification,
and captured I/O.
If the pair has no run ([[run=None]]), we return an
empty string.

<<progsnap2 functions>>=
def format_run_latex(pair, resources, gaps=None):
    """Format the run part of a pair as LaTeX.

    Returns a string with script info and I/O in a
    ``minted`` block.  Returns empty string if no run.
    """
    if pair["run"] is None:
        return ""

    run = pair["run"]
    parts = []

    script = run.get("X-Script", "")
    result = run.get("ProgramResult", "")
    exit_code = run.get("X-ExitCode", "")
    gap = gaps.get(run["EventID"]) if gaps else None

    info_parts = []
    elapsed = format_elapsed(gap)
    if elapsed:
        info_parts.append(
            f"Time since last run: {elapsed}"
        )
    if script:
        info_parts.append(
            f"Script: \\texttt{{{_latex_escape(script)}}}"
        )
    if result:
        info_parts.append(f"Result: {result}")
    if exit_code:
        info_parts.append(
            f"Exit code: {exit_code}"
        )
    if info_parts:
        parts.append(" \\\\\n".join(info_parts))
        parts.append("")

    <<format exception info>>

    io_text = format_io_latex(
        pair["io"], resources,
    )
    if io_text:
        parts.append("\\begin{minted}{text}")
        parts.append(io_text)
        parts.append("\\end{minted}")

    return "\n".join(parts)
@

If the run produced an exception, we show it in its
own [[minted]] block so the reader can see what error
the student encountered.

<<format exception info>>=
exc_type = run.get("X-ExceptionType", "")
exc_msg = run.get("X-ExceptionMessage", "")
if exc_type:
    exc_str = exc_type
    if exc_msg:
        exc_str += f": {exc_msg}"
    parts.append("\\begin{minted}{pytb}")
    parts.append(exc_str)
    parts.append("\\end{minted}")
    parts.append("")
